 &lt;div&gt;
 Environmental maps are usually trained on monitoring networks that cluster where sites are&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;easy to reach, so split conformal intervals are too narrow where nobody monitored. We ask&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;whether the dissimilarity index (DI) of the area-of-applicability framework, already routine in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;spatial machine learning, can be used to condition conformal calibration. A DI-scaled residual&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;score (DI-normalized) and conformalized quantile regression within DI bins (DI-CQR) are&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;compared with split conformal prediction, conformalized quantile regression (CQR), localized&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;conformal prediction with Guan’s adjusted level, and weighted conformal prediction using the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;exact inclusion probabilities of the monitoring design. In a simulation scored at every unmonitored&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;cell, severe covariate-driven bias left split conformal covering 0.34 of held-out cells and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;0.14 in the worst region. DI-CQR raised these to 0.69 and 0.38, with finite intervals everywhere.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Oracle-weighted conformal prediction covered more but was unbounded at 78% of cells; localized&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;conformal prediction was unbounded at 20% and covered less. When sites followed a road field&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;hidden from the model, DI-CQR lifted worst-region coverage from 0.29 to 0.54 under severe&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;bias, while estimated weights made split conformal’s interval score worse. On 21,687 LUCAS&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;topsoil sites DI-CQR raised worst-region coverage from 0.57 to 0.78 at CQR’s interval score; DInormalized&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;intervals were 3.2 times wider. No method reached nominal worst-region coverage&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;under strong simulated bias without unbounded intervals. DI-CQR is a practical option when&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;the selection mechanism is unknown, because it sets how much wider intervals should be under&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;extrapolation without a model of the monitoring design.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

 &lt;div&gt;
 This paper integrates two empirical regime classifications from the author's series — the HABITUATED regime (Takagi, 2026a) and the HIGH-DOWN regime of a macro fear score (Takagi, 2026b) — into a unified three-phase framework for U.S. equity drawdown periods.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Across the S&amp;amp;P 500 sample (January 1990 to April 2026, 9,117 trading days), the two regimes are near-disjoint at the daily resolution yet sequentially linked: every one of the ten HABITUATED episode end-points is followed by a HIGH-DOWN activation. This motivates a three-phase classification — Phase I (HABITUATED), Phase II (Healing Period), Phase III (Recovery) — whose conditional 250-day forward-return distributions are sharply distinct, with non-overlapping bootstrap intervals between Phase I (mean -21.2%) and Phase III (mean +22.9%).
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 We validate the conditional intervals with split conformal prediction and examine their robustness, including the limits of out-of-episode generalisation. The framework is descriptive throughout: it characterises conditional return distributions and proposes no forecast or trading rule. The structure rests on two historical episodes (the dot-com collapse and the global financial crisis), and we are explicit about the scope this implies.
&lt;/div&gt;

Let $(M^n,g_0)$ be a smooth compact Riemannian manifold of dimension $n\geq 3$ with smooth non-empty boundary $\partial M$. Let $Γ\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ be a symmetric convex cone and $f$ a symmetric defining function for $Γ$ satisfying standard assumptions. Denoting by $A_{g_u}$ the Schouten tensor of a conformal metric $g_u = u^{-2}g_0$, we show that the associated fully nonlinear Loewner-Nirenberg problem
  \begin{align*}
  \begin{cases}
  f(λ(-g_u^{-1}A_{g_u})) = \frac{1}{2}, \quad λ(-g_u^{-1}A_{g_u})\inΓ& \text{on }M\backslash \partial M \newline
  u = 0 & \text{on }\partial M
  \end{cases}
  \end{align*}
  admits a solution if $μ_Γ^+ > 1-δ$, where $μ_Γ^+$ is defined by $(-μ_Γ^+,1,\dots,1)\in\partialΓ$ and $δ>0$ is a constant depending on certain geometric data. In particular, we solve the $σ_k$-Loewner-Nirenberg problem for all $k\leq \frac{n}{2}$, which extends recent work of the authors to include the important threshold case $k=\frac{n}{2}$. In the process, we establish that the fully nonlinear Loewner-Nirenberg problem and corresponding Dirichlet boundary value problem with positive boundary data admit solutions if there exists a conformal metric $g\in[g_0]$ such that $λ(-g^{-1}A_g)\inΓ$ on $M$; these latter results require no assumption on $μ_Γ^+$ and are new when $(1,0,\dots,0)\in\partialΓ$.
